.TH EUCLID-CLI-EES-SUBSCRIBE-EVENTS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ees-subscribe-events \- register a durable subscription to euclid events
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ees subscribe-events
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-event\-types | \-e
.I LIST
[\fB\-\-filter\fR|\fB\-f\fR \fIJSON\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Registers a subscription under
.IR NAME .
From then on every matching event is stored for that name whether or not
anything is connected to receive it, and stays there until it is
acknowledged \- which is what lets an application restart, or be down for
an hour, without missing what happened meanwhile.
.PP
The name is the identity of the consumer, not of a process. Two
consumers that use different names each get their own copy of an event,
so both can act on it; two instances that share a name share the work,
because a claim is atomic and only one of them gets any given event.
.PP
Subscribing again with the same name and event type replaces that
subscription's filter rather than adding a second one, so redeploying an
application with a narrower filter really does narrow it.
.PP
The subscription is scoped to the caller's account: an event whose
payload names a different account is never stored for it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Subscriber name events are stored and claimed under. Required.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-event\-types " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated event types, e.g.
.IR esm.object.created,esm.object.deleted .
Required. See
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1)
for the types that exist.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-filter " " \fIJSON\fR
JSON object of payload fields that must match exactly. Omitted means
every event of these types.
.SH FILTERS
A filter is applied when the event is published, not when it is
delivered, so a subscriber's backlog is proportional to what it asked
for rather than to how busy the installation is.
.PP
ESM's object events carry
.IR bucketName ,
.IR key ,
.I prefix
and
.I directory
for exactly this purpose:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"bucketName":"inbox"}                    one bucket
{"bucketName":"inbox","prefix":"in/"}     one "directory" of it
{"directory":false}                       no FTP MKD markers
.fi
.RE
.PP
A key is a path by convention only, which is why
.I prefix
is a field of its own: it is the key up to and including its last
slash, so it can be matched exactly like anything else.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The subscription was registered. All of the subscriber's subscriptions
are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, a
.B \-\-filter
that is not a JSON object, no session, or the server rejected the
request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Every upload into one bucket, excluding directory markers:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees subscribe-events \e
    --name invoice-import \e
    --event-types esm.object.created \e
    --filter '{"bucketName":"inbox","directory":false}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Everything that happens to one directory of one bucket:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees subscribe-events \e
    --name report-index \e
    --event-types esm.object.created,esm.object.updated,esm.object.deleted \e
    --filter '{"bucketName":"reports","prefix":"2026/"}'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-receive-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-list-subscriptions (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-unsubscribe-events (1)
